Why does HashSet not allow duplicate elements internally?
You said:
“internally it uses hashtable which contains non duplicate elements”
This is directionally right, but interviewers will ask:
👉 WHY duplicates are prevented, not just that they are.

Correct & interview-ready answer (use this)
“HashSet does not allow duplicate elements because it is internally backed by a HashMap,
where elements are stored as keys, and HashMap does not allow duplicate keys—any duplicate element replaces the existing entry instead of being added.”

🧠 Internal execution (VERY IMPORTANT — lock this in)
When you do:
Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add("A");
set.add("A");

What really happens internally:
HashMap<String, Object> map;
map.put("A", DUMMY);
map.put("A", DUMMY); // replaces, not adds

HashSet stores elements as keys
Value is a constant dummy object
Duplicate key → value replaced → no new entry
👉 That’s why no duplicates

🔑 One-line mental rule (remember forever)
HashSet = HashMap keys only
If HashMap can’t have duplicate keys → HashSet can’t have duplicate elements.

