Given an array of integers, find the first element that appears more than once.

🔧 Refinement (this is subtle but critical)
You answered:
Pattern: seen-before + order + frequency
Data structure: LinkedHashMap
⚠️ Small correction
For “first element that appears more than once”, we actually do NOT need frequency.
Why?
The moment an element appears again, it becomes repeating.
We don’t need to know how many times, just that it repeated.

✅ Correct final answers (interview-accurate)
1️⃣ Pattern
Seen-Before + Order

2️⃣ Data structure
LinkedHashSet OR HashMap + index
Best & cleanest:
✅ LinkedHashSet

3️⃣ Why
Because we need to track seen elements while preserving insertion order, so the first element that repeats can be identified correctly.

🧠 Why LinkedHashMap is NOT required here
LinkedHashMap is needed when:
you must count frequency
or find non-repeating

Here:
repeating is detected on second occurrence
no counting needed
So LinkedHashSet is simpler and better.

🧩 Let’s LOCK this pattern with ALL 4 CASES (VERY IMPORTANT)

CASE 1️⃣: Any duplicate exists? (yes/no)
Pattern: Seen-Before
DS: HashSet
for (int x : arr) {
    if (!set.add(x)) {
        System.out.println("Duplicate exists");
        break;
    }
}

CASE 2️⃣: First repeating element
Pattern: Seen-Before + Order
DS: LinkedHashSet
Set<Integer> seen = new LinkedHashSet<>();
for (int x : arr) {
    if (!seen.add(x)) {
        System.out.println("First repeating: " + x);
        break;
    }
}

CASE 3️⃣: First non-repeating character
Pattern: Frequency + Order
DS: LinkedHashMap
Map<Character, Integer> map = new LinkedHashMap<>();
for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
    map.put(c, map.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 1);
}

for (var e : map.entrySet()) {
    if (e.getValue() == 1) {
        System.out.println("First non-repeating: " + e.getKey());
        break;
    }
}

CASE 4️⃣: Frequency count
Pattern: Frequency
DS: HashMap
map.put(x, map.getOrDefault(x, 0) + 1);

