Given a string, find the longest substring without repeating characters.

1️⃣ Pattern
Variable-length Sliding Window + Seen-Before
✅ Correct.

2️⃣ Data structure
HashSet
(or HashMap if index tracking is needed)
✅ Correct choice for the basic version.

3️⃣ Why
Because we need to dynamically expand and shrink a window while ensuring all characters inside the window are unique (no repetition).
That’s a strong explanation.

🧠 Important refinement (this is subtle but powerful)
There are two valid data-structure choices, depending on what the problem asks:

🔹 Use HashSet when:
You only need the length
You only care about uniqueness
You’re okay removing characters one by one

while (set.contains(s.charAt(right))) {
    set.remove(s.charAt(left));
    left++;
}

🔹 Use HashMap<Character, Integer> when:
You want faster jumps of left
You want to track last seen index
You want cleaner logic for large inputs

if (map.containsKey(ch) && map.get(ch) >= left) {
    left = map.get(ch) + 1;
}

🧠 Interview insight:
Both are correct. HashMap version is often considered more optimal.

